1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--extensive-reading","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/extensive-reading","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/extensive-reading","sourceHash":"b5ed47de043545a40198476e763d71b7550b45eb0d9ec8567a935ef1f989c3b4","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T16:52:55.700Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Extensive Reading","zh":"泛读"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Extensive reading means reading large amounts of self-selected material in your target language, primarily for enjoyment. It's not study — it's reading the way you might in your native language: choosing books you're interested in, reading at a comfortable pace, and not stopping to analyze every sentence.","zh":"泛读是指主要为了享受阅读而大量阅读自己选择的目标语言材料。这不是学习，而是像阅读母语内容一样去阅读：选择自己感兴趣的书，以舒适的速度阅读，不停下来分析每一个句子。"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"In the Refold method, extensive reading is introduced as an option in Phase 2 and becomes increasingly important from Phase 3 onward. It's one of the most powerful ways to build deep, intuitive knowledge of a language. Once you're in Phase 5, you should be reading a lot.","zh":"在 Refold 方法中，阶段 2 会把泛读作为一个可选项目引入；从阶段 3 开始，它会变得越来越重要。泛读是建立深刻、直觉性语言知识最有效的方法之一。进入阶段 5 后，你应该进行大量阅读。"}},{"id":"why-reading-matters","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Why Reading Matters","zh":"为什么阅读很重要"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Reading exposes you to far more vocabulary than conversation or TV. Novels typically draw from around 8,000 word families, while everyday conversation uses only about 5,000. Reading is where you encounter the words that take you from functional to truly fluent.","zh":"与对话或电视节目相比，阅读能让你接触到多得多的词汇。小说通常会使用约 8000 个词族，而日常对话只会使用约 5000 个。通过阅读遇到的词汇，能让你从基本够用迈向真正流利。"}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Beyond vocabulary, reading gives you sustained exposure to grammar patterns in context. Complex structures that fly by in spoken language are laid out on the page where you can process them at your own pace. Written language also tends to be more fully formed than speech — complete sentences, varied structures, and more sophisticated expression. This doesn't make it \"better\" than spoken language, but it gives your brain clearer examples to learn from, which in turn helps you process the messier, less complete patterns of real speech.","zh":"除了词汇以外，阅读还能让你在语境中持续接触语法模式。在口语中一闪而过的复杂结构，会清楚地呈现在书页上，让你按照自己的节奏处理。书面语言通常也比口语更加完整——句子完整、结构多样、表达也更精细。这并不意味着书面语言比口语“更好”，但它能为大脑提供更清晰的学习范例，进而帮助你处理真实口语中更零散、不完整的模式。"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Perhaps the most consistent finding in the research is that extensive reading dramatically improves motivation and attitude toward learning. Learners who read regularly report renewed interest in the language, greater engagement, and less of the \"grammar slog\" feeling that can come from pure study.","zh":"研究中最一致的发现或许是：泛读能显著提高学习动力，改善学习态度。经常阅读的学习者表示，他们重新燃起了对这门语言的兴趣，投入度更高，也较少感到纯粹学习带来的那种“苦熬语法”的疲惫。"}},{"id":"finding-the-right-level","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Finding the Right Level","zh":"找到合适的难度"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You want material where you know roughly 95-98% of the words.","zh":"你要选择自己大致认识其中 95%–98% 词汇的材料。"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A simple test: read a page and count the words you don't know. Zero unknown words is probably too easy; four or five per page is probably too hard. One to three unknown words per page is the sweet spot — enough to learn from, not so many that you lose the thread of the story.","zh":"有一个简单的测试方法：读一页，并数一数不认识的词。一个生词都没有，可能太简单；每页有四五个生词，可能太难。每页有一到三个生词最为合适——既能从中学到东西，又不会多到让你跟不上故事脉络。"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Keep in mind that difficulty varies within a book. A page describing a new setting might have more unknown words than a dialogue scene.","zh":"请记住，同一本书中的难度也会有所变化。描写新场景的页面可能比对话场景包含更多生词。"}},{"id":"getting-started","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Getting Started","zh":"如何开始"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"If you haven't read a novel in your target language before, start small and build up.","zh":"如果你从未读过目标语言小说，可以先从短小简单的内容开始，再逐步增加难度和篇幅。"}},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Graded readers</strong> exist for most commonly learned languages. These are written specifically for learners, using controlled vocabulary. They can feel a bit dry at the lowest levels, but they get more engaging as you progress, and they're an excellent bridge into native material.","zh":"大多数常见的学习语言都有<strong>分级读物</strong>。这类读物专门为学习者编写，使用受控词汇。最低级别的内容可能略显枯燥，但随着水平提高，内容会越来越有趣；它们也是过渡到母语者材料的绝佳桥梁。"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Familiar stories</strong> work well. Many learners start with Harry Potter or similar series in translation — you already know the plot, so you can follow along even when the language is challenging. The simpler vocabulary of children's and young adult series is a bonus.","zh":"<strong>熟悉的故事</strong>也很适合。许多学习者会从译成目标语言的《哈利·波特》或类似系列开始——你已经知道故事情节，所以即使语言有难度，也能继续读下去。儿童和青少年系列作品的词汇较为简单，这也是额外的优势。"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Children's chapter books</strong> are short, highly serialized (often 20+ books in a series), use recurring vocabulary, and are written for native-speaking kids who are just learning to read independently. They build you up to harder material quickly.","zh":"<strong>儿童章节书</strong>篇幅短、系列化程度高（一个系列通常有 20 多本），会反复使用相同的词汇，而且是为刚开始独立阅读的母语儿童编写的。它们能帮助你快速过渡到更难的材料。"}},{"id":"segment-0016","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Short-form reading</strong> like articles or blog posts can also work. They're self-contained, lower commitment, and let you practice reading without the stamina demands of a full novel.","zh":"<strong>短篇阅读材料</strong>也可以，例如文章或博客帖子。这类内容自成一体，需要投入的时间较少，也能让你练习阅读，而不必具备读完整部长篇小说所需的耐力。"}},{"id":"the-super-mario-bros-approach","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"The Super Mario Bros Approach","zh":"超级马力欧兄弟式方法"}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"If you're working with limited material (a small language, few available books, or one specific book you really want to read) try restarting from the beginning each day. Like playing a video game before save files existed, you start from the beginning and see how far you get.","zh":"如果可用材料有限（所学语言规模较小、能找到的书很少，或你特别想读某一本书），可以尝试每天从头开始读。就像在游戏还没有存档功能的年代玩电子游戏一样，每次都从头开始，看看自己能推进多远。"}},{"id":"segment-0019","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Each day you'll understand a little more, remember more words, and push further into the text. Eventually you'll be able to pick up from the middle and keep going. This approach works especially well for low-resource languages.","zh":"每天你都会多理解一点、记住更多词，并在文本中推进得更远。最终，你将能够从中间接着读下去。这种方法对资源较少的语言尤其有效。"}},{"id":"finding-books","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Finding Books","zh":"寻找书籍"}},{"id":"segment-0021","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Search for local ebook platforms in your target language — many countries have their own retailers or subscription services that offer far better selection than international platforms. Storytel, for example, supports many languages with both books and audiobooks, though access may be region-locked.","zh":"搜索目标语言所在地区的本地电子书平台。许多国家都有自己的零售商或订阅服务，提供的选择远胜于国际平台。例如，Storytel 提供多种语言的电子书和有声书，不过某些内容可能存在地区访问限制。"}},{"id":"segment-0022","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Libraries — even foreign ones — are a valuable and often overlooked resource. Some countries (like Sweden and Norway) make it relatively easy to get a library card and access ebooks even from abroad. Ask native speakers or fellow learners how they get their books.","zh":"图书馆——即使是国外的图书馆——也是一种宝贵却常被忽视的资源。有些国家（例如瑞典和挪威）允许身在国外的人较为轻松地办理借书证并使用电子书。可以向母语者或其他学习者询问他们如何获取书籍。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0024","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The benefits of extensive reading for language acquisition are well-supported by research. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/tesq.157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nakanishi (2015)</a> conducted a meta-analysis of 34 empirical studies and found consistent positive effects on reading proficiency. <a href=\"http://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/the_power_of_reading.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen (2004)</a> reviewed hundreds of studies and argued that self-selected free reading has strong effects on reading, writing, vocabulary, grammar, and spelling across both first and second languages.","zh":"泛读对语言习得的益处得到了充分的研究支持。<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/tesq.157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nakanishi（2015）</a>对 34 项实证研究进行了元分析，发现泛读对阅读能力具有稳定的积极影响。<a href=\"http://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/the_power_of_reading.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen（2004）</a>回顾了数百项研究，并指出，无论对第一语言还是第二语言，自主选择的自由阅读都能显著促进阅读、写作、词汇、语法和拼写能力。"}},{"id":"segment-0025","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The claim that reading predicts grammar acquisition — even for notoriously difficult structures — is supported by <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1075/itl.121-122.02sto\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Stokes, Krashen &amp; Kartchner (1998)</a>, who found that the amount of free reading in Spanish was the only significant predictor of subjunctive competence among university students. Length of residence, formal study, and specific study of the subjunctive were not significant predictors.","zh":"<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1075/itl.121-122.02sto\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Stokes、Krashen 和 Kartchner（1998）</a>的研究支持了“阅读可以预测语法习得”的观点，即使是出了名难学的结构也不例外。他们发现，在大学生群体中，西班牙语自由阅读量是唯一能显著预测虚拟式掌握程度的因素。居住时长、正规学习经历以及专门学习虚拟式的时间，都不是显著的预测因素。"}},{"id":"segment-0026","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The vocabulary coverage figures come from <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3138/cmlr.63.1.59\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nation (2006)</a>. The recommended comprehension threshold of 95-98% known words for extensive reading draws on <a href=\"https://nflrc.hawaii.edu/rfl/item/109\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hu &amp; Nation (2000)</a> and <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-349-12396-4_12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Laufer (1992)</a>, who established that learners need high lexical coverage for unassisted reading comprehension. <a href=\"https://www.routledge.com/The-Routledge-Handbook-of-Vocabulary-Studies/Webb/p/book/9781032401270\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Webb (2020)</a> further showed that incidental vocabulary learning through reading is a primary driver of vocabulary growth, with learners picking up words naturally through repeated exposure in meaningful contexts.","zh":"词汇覆盖率数据来自<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3138/cmlr.63.1.59\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nation（2006）</a>。泛读时建议达到 95%–98% 已知词汇这一理解门槛，依据的是<a href=\"https://nflrc.hawaii.edu/rfl/item/109\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hu 和 Nation（2000）</a>以及<a href=\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-349-12396-4_12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Laufer（1992）</a>的研究；他们证实，学习者需要较高的词汇覆盖率，才能在没有辅助的情况下理解阅读内容。<a href=\"https://www.routledge.com/The-Routledge-Handbook-of-Vocabulary-Studies/Webb/p/book/9781032401270\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Webb（2020）</a>进一步证明，通过阅读进行的附带词汇学习是词汇量增长的主要驱动力；学习者会在有意义的语境中反复接触词汇，从而自然地掌握它们。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
